(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "260726-misogi-macos-hygiene-console")
  (:adr/title "ai-gftd-misogi — reversible macOS hygiene console (CleanMyMac-class cleaner as a cybersecurity product)")
  (:adr/status "active")
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/topic "cybersecurity")
  (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-26")
  (:adr/body "# Decision\n\nCreate `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi` (禊 — ritual purification by washing) as the desktop hygiene console of the `ai-gftd-*` cybersecurity series, built on `kotoba-lang/shell` as its native host.\n\nThe product is CleanMyMac-class in surface (find junk, reclaim disk, uninstall leftovers, review startup items) but is designed as a **security product**, not a cleaner with a security badge. Three commitments make that difference real and testable:\n\n1. **Two scored axes, not one.** Every observed item carries both a `kegare` score (穢れ — junk: reclaimable bytes × staleness × safety) and a `tsumi` score (罪 — threat: persistence, signature, provenance, exposure). A 40 GB stale Xcode cache and a 12 KB unsigned LaunchAgent are both findings; they rank on different axes and are never collapsed into one \"junk\" number the way consumer cleaners do.\n2. **Deny-by-default admission.** Nothing enters a removal plan because a heuristic liked it. A candidate must (a) sit under an explicitly allowlisted root, (b) fail no protected-path predicate, and (c) be matched by a named rule that attaches its own evidence. Every plan line is traceable to a rule id.\n3. **No deletion. Ever.** `apply` moves items into a per-run quarantine vault with a full restore manifest; `restore` puts them back at their exact original paths with mode and mtime. Reclaiming the space is a separate, later, explicit `purge` gesture on a vault that has aged past a retention floor. This is simultaneously the safety-floor requirement (no destructive irreversible removal) and the correct engineering design for a tool that removes things a heuristic chose.\n\n# Why a new repo, and where it sits in the series\n\n`ai-gftd-mamori` is the *cross-platform, off-device* privacy map: which company receives which data from which app. `ai-gftd-misogi` is its *on-device, local-only* counterpart: what is actually on this machine, what persists across reboot, what holds TCC grants, and what can be reversibly removed. They share the vocabulary of recipients and permission classes and are intentionally not merged — mamori's data is owner-scoped cloud state, misogi's never leaves the device.\n\n`ai-gftd-kakure` (personal-data erasure) and `ai-gftd-hakken` (discovery) do not overlap: kakure erases records held by third parties, misogi quarantines files held locally.\n\n# Architecture\n\n```\nsrc/misogi/*.cljc          pure, IO-free core — the whole product's judgment\n  taxonomy   controlled vocabulary: item classes, persistence classes, TCC classes,\n             severity bands, evidence kinds\n  rules      the rule table as data (kegare rules + tsumi rules), each with id,\n             matcher, evidence extractor, base severity\n  score      kegare/tsumi scoring from rule hits + observed facts\n  policy     deny-by-default path admission (allow roots, protected predicates)\n  plan       facts + policy + rules -> ordered, reversible plan\n  ledger     append-only receipt construction\n  view       hiccup views (dual-render: kotoba-ui ->page HTML, and kotoba:dom ops)\n\nsrc/misogi/host/*.cljs     nbb — the only place IO exists\n  fs         bounded directory walk + stat (never follows symlinks out of root)\n  probe      launchd / login items / config profiles / codesign / spctl / xattr\n  vault      quarantine move + restore + purge, with manifest\n  cli        bin/misogi entry\n\nmisogi.app.kotoba.edn      kotoba-shell app manifest (native macOS window)\nmisogi.plugin.edn          kotoba-shell plugin: the provider commands misogi needs\n```\n\nThe pure core takes **observed facts** and returns **plans and scores**. It cannot read a disk, spawn a process, or delete anything — that is what makes the rule table auditable and the tests real.\n\n# Host surface: why a plugin, and why no listening socket\n\n`kotoba-lang/shell`'s provider catalog (`aiueos_provider_catalog.edn`) ships 9 provider families: ledger, fs-app-data, notification, clipboard, http-fetch, keychain, contacts, calendar, webauthn. `fs/*` is app-data scoped read/write/append text — there is no directory walk, no stat, no move, no process inventory. misogi therefore declares a kotoba-shell **plugin** (`misogi.plugin.edn`) contributing three read-mostly provider families:\n\n- `misogi/fs-survey`   — `fs/walk`, `fs/stat` (read-only, root-bounded)\n- `misogi/persistence` — `persistence/list`, `signature/verify` (read-only)\n- `misogi/vault`       — `vault/quarantine`, `vault/restore`, `vault/purge` (the only mutating family; move-only)\n\nThe native window dispatches UI actions through `KOTOBA_SHELL_ACTION_BIN` (stdin event -> stdout receipt), **not** through the `data-endpoint` HTTP path the mangaka exemplar uses. A security tool should not open a listening port on the machine it is auditing, and the action-bin path is already supported by `bin/kotoba-shell-app-runtime`.\n\n# Runtime placement\n\nThe repo rule orders runtimes kotoba wasm > clojurewasm > ClojureScript > nbb, with JVM last. misogi's first-class execution path is **nbb** for the CLI, survey, vault, and HTML report — the pure core is `.cljc` and runs there unchanged.\n\nThe native-window path is the exception and it is not misogi's choice: `kotoba-shell app run` evaluates an app entry with `clojure -M -e \"(require 'ns)(prn (ns/start))\"`, so the shell's app contract is JVM-hosted today. misogi ships a thin `src/misogi/app.clj` shim that requires the same `.cljc` core and returns `:kotoba.app/surface-ops`. No product logic lives in the shim. Moving the shell app entry off the JVM is upstream work in `kotoba-lang/shell`, tracked as a follow-up, not something misogi routes around by duplicating logic.\n\n# UI\n\nViews are pure `.cljc` hiccup and render two ways from one source: `kotoba-ui.core/->page` for the shareable HTML audit report (design-quality scored, per ADR-2607132300), and a hiccup -> `kotoba:dom` op lowering for the native window, which the macOS host renders with the `liquid-glass__panel` / `liquid-glass__button` classes it already understands. No raw hex, no ad-hoc font sizes: one theme map, HIG text styles (ADR-2607122200).\n\n**The two renderers are not equal fidelity, and the native one is the lesser.** Measured 2026-07-26 against this machine: the HTML report scores 100.00 on the deterministic design-quality HIG/WCAG audit (control: a deliberately bad page scores 30.96, so the auditor discriminates). The native window renders the same view correctly but through the shell's AppKit subset — `kotoba-ui.shell/grid` collapses to a vertical stack because the native renderer has no grid, and long hero titles clip rather than wrap. These are limits of `kotoba-lang/shell`'s macOS renderer, not of the view, and they are upstream work.\n\nThe shell's `--web-url` WKWebView path would give the native window full design-system fidelity, and was evaluated. It is rejected for now on two grounds: `WKWebView.load(URLRequest:)` does not load `file://` (it needs `loadFileURL:allowingReadAccessTo:`), so the report would have to be served over loopback HTTP — a listening socket on the machine being audited, which contradicts the action-bin decision above — and the shell's app runtime disables surface commits in web mode, so the action -> re-render loop would need a separate design. Revisit if the shell gains a `loadFileURL` path.\n\n# LLM containment\n\nAn advisor model may annotate findings in plain language. It may **not** author, reorder, or admit a plan line — plans come only from the deterministic engine, and the action bin rejects any plan whose lines lack a rule id and a policy admission receipt. This keeps the actor-pattern containment property without making the product depend on a model being available or correct.\n\n# Verification (2026-07-26, first implementation)\n\n66 tests / 168 assertions pass under nbb, including a real-filesystem quarantine -> restore round trip (temp tree, `MISOGI_HOME` redirected) that asserts mode and mtime survive.\n\nRun read-only against this machine: 209 findings in ~27 s — 26 tsumi, 183 kegare, 1.3 GB reclaimable. Full CLI pipeline exercised end to end against a misogi-owned fixture inside a genuinely allowlisted root: dry run changed nothing, `apply --yes` moved it, `vault` listed it held, `purge --yes` refused with `within-retention` at 0 days, `restore` returned it byte- and mtime-identical, and the append-only ledger recorded survey/plan/apply/refusal/restore.\n\nThe first real run produced four false-positive classes that unit tests could not have found, all now fixed with regression tests:\n\n1. **Every third-party root daemon scored critical.** `execFileSync` only exposes stderr on its error object, and `codesign -dv` writes its whole report to stderr and exits 0 — so every signature verdict came back `:unknown`. Fixed by using `spawnSync`. This was a real defect, not a tuning issue: the tool was reporting confidently on evidence it had never actually read.\n2. **The entire local toolchain was flagged as concealment.** `hidden-program?` matched any dot-directory, so every `.venv/bin/*`, `~/.local/bin/*` and `~/.cargo/bin/*` autostart entry read as hidden. Now a known-toolchain allowlist applies; `/tmp` and unrecognised dot-directories still match.\n3. **Empty launchd stubs scored as unsigned root daemons.** Several uninstallers leave a plist with no `Program` at all. A plist that runs nothing is no longer a persistence fact.\n4. **Coverage lost sources.** Two roots feeding one surface overwrote each other, so `:launch-agents` printed \"0 jobs in /Library/LaunchAgents\" while 37 findings from `~/Library/LaunchAgents` sat in the same report. `misogi.coverage` now degrades statuses instead of overwriting — a denial can never be masked by a later success.\n\nSeparately, the native smoke run caught a lowering bug that no unit test covered: kotoba-ui slots (`nav-bar :trailing`) hand back a *vector of sibling elements*, which the hiccup->ops walker read as `[tag & children]` and serialized an entire button into a tag name. Fragments are now distinguished from elements and spliced.\n\nThe recurring lesson is that the rules that matter here cannot be validated against synthetic facts. Every one of these was invisible until the tool ran against a machine that actually had 37 LaunchAgents on it.\n\n# Gap closure round 2 (2026-07-26)\n\nA gap audit after the first landing found several defects that the first verification pass had missed, all now closed. 78 core tests / 203 assertions plus 8 view tests pass.\n\n1. **The TCC survey read the wrong database.** Only `~/Library/.../TCC.db` was read, but Full Disk Access, Screen Recording, Accessibility and Input Monitoring — the four grants this taxonomy weights heaviest — live *only* in the system database at `/Library/Application Support/com.apple.TCC/TCC.db`. The survey would have reported `[complete]` while omitting every high-impact grant. Both databases are now read and merged per client, with per-source coverage, so a denial on either is visible separately. This was the one place coverage could actively mislead rather than merely under-report.\n2. **Two rules could never fire.** `:kegare/duplicate` depended on `:sha256`, which the host never computed — the rule shipped with a passing unit test that supplied the hash production never produced. `:tsumi/listening-unsigned` depended on a signature verdict for a process identified only by `lsof`'s truncated command name, and a name cannot be code-signed. Fixed by bounded content hashing (64 MB cap, enabled where duplicates actually accumulate) and by resolving pids to real executable paths via `ps`. Both now fire on real data: 4 duplicates found, and 10/10 listening processes resolve to a signed-checkable path.\n3. **`:find` mode was unreachable.** `survey`'s `case` had no branch for it, so a root declaring `:mode :find` silently produced nothing.\n4. **`vault gc`** added. The vault previously only grew; the sole way to reclaim its space was purging runs one id at a time. Same gates as `purge!`.\n5. **The action bin was untested.** `bin/misogi-action` — the window's entire contract — had never been executed. It now has end-to-end tests against the real script, asserting among other things that `purge` is refused from the window and that a malformed event still produces a receipt rather than silence.\n6. **`misogi.plugin.edn` `:notes` was a literal `(str ...)` list.** EDN is data; `kotoba-shell plugin check` was echoing back `[\"str\" \"...\" \"...\"]`.\n\n**One item in that audit was my own error, in both directions.** I recorded orphan detection as having near-zero recall because it only classified reverse-DNS directory names. Relaxing it to match plain app names raised orphan findings from a handful to **131 of 211 directories** — and inspection showed most were live command-line tool state (`virtualenv`, `pypoetry`, `deno-wasmbuild`, `cloud-code`), which has no `.app` and never did. \"No installed app matches\" is not evidence that something is a leftover when it was never an app. The relaxation was reverted in favour of a stricter gate than the original: only reverse-DNS names are classified at all, with better matching within that set (an app named after the id's last segment now counts as the owner). Result: 9 orphans, all genuinely reverse-DNS. The original code was closer to right than the audit claimed, and the recall limit is now a documented trade — a missed leftover costs disk, a false one points at a working tool's state.\n\n# UI: moved to the Digital Agency Design System (2026-07-26)\n\nOwner directive mid-implementation: use `kotoba-lang/jp-go-digital-design-system` (DADS). The console was rebuilt on it, replacing the kotoba-ui glass/HIG stack entirely — the generated document contains zero `liquid-glass__*` or `hig-*` classes.\n\n**kotoba-uiux opt-out reason** (required of DADS adopters by ADR-2607141915): primarily the owner directive. It also fits the product: misogi asks a person to make removal decisions about their own machine on evidence it gathered, and DADS is a plain, high-legibility civic visual language with no marketing gloss. The glass stack read as product chrome, the wrong register for asking a judgement rather than selling one. This is a weaker justification than cloud-itonami's (ADR-2607261600), whose faces implement statutory duties; it is recorded as a directive plus a fit, not as a policy necessity.\n\n**Light mode only**, as in ADR-2607261600 decision 2 — upstream ships no dark palette and hand-rolling one would diverge from the design system. Zero `prefers-color-scheme` rules in the output.\n\n**The migration was scored at every step, and it initially regressed.** The glass version measured 100.00 on the deterministic design-quality audit; the first DADS render measured **65.34**, losing safe-area (0.13), tap-targets (0.13), responsive (0.07) and color-scheme (0.06). The vendored DADS subset does not carry those properties. They were fixed **upstream in jp-go-dds** rather than patched locally, because the gap belongs to the design-system layer and cloud-itonami's DADS faces had it too — `ext-media` now supplies a `(pointer:coarse)` 44px tap target, `env(safe-area-inset-*)` padding, a `max-width` breakpoint, and a CSS `color-scheme: light` declaration. Back to 100.00. `jp-go-dds.core/button` also gained an `:attrs` passthrough, without which a consumer needing a `data-action` hook had to hand-roll a button and duplicate DADS button CSS.\n\n# Surfaces: which one actually renders, and what is not landed\n\nThe question \"is this running in the shell webview?\" was answered by measurement: `kotoba-shell app run` never passes `--web-url`, and the host emits `{\"surface\":\"kotoba:dom\",\"runtime\":\"native-appkit\"}`. The native window was the AppKit surface — which is why grids stacked and headings clipped, and which cannot style `dads-*` classes at all.\n\nThe webview surface does work and was demonstrated (`{\"surface\":\"webview\"}`, a 100.00-scoring DADS console in a native window). **But it is not landable today**, for two independent reasons:\n\n1. The WKWebView surface in `kotoba-lang/shell` is **unpushed local WIP** — three commits ahead of `origin/rescue/wip-20260718`, whose published copy contains no `WKWebView` at all. Publishing another session's unpushed work is not mine to do.\n2. `WKWebView.load(URLRequest:)` refuses `file://`; it needs `loadFileURL(_:allowingReadAccessTo:)`. Without that the only way to use the web surface is loopback HTTP — a listening socket on the machine being audited, contradicting this ADR's action-bin decision. The fix is kept as `patches/kotoba-shell-webview-file-url.diff` in the misogi repo.\n\nSo `bin/misogi-app` opens the DADS console in the **browser** by default — it needs nothing but this repo — and `--shell-window` detects whether the shell checkout can load a `file://` URL, falling back to the browser rather than opening a blank window. The webview surface also has no JS bridge, so in that mode the console is read-only and actions run from the CLI; closing that needs a `WKScriptMessageHandler` upstream.\n\n# Single-screen redesign (2026-07-26)\n\nOwner direction: make it a single-screen app, iPad-legible. The report layout was replaced by a grouped left rail of destinations, one focused pane at a time, and one primary action per pane — the shape of the category-sidebar cleaners this product is compared to. Copy is Japanese: DADS is the Japanese government design system, and matching the language is part of matching the register.\n\n**Navigation carries no JavaScript.** Panes switch on `:target`; the active rail item is styled through `:has()`. That is a requirement, not a preference — the webview surface has no JS bridge, and the same document must work when double-clicked. The visibility and active-state CSS is generated from the `nav` list so it cannot drift from the destination set, and a test asserts a pane exists for every rail entry.\n\nFindings are routed to panes by the evidence they actually carry (`:persistence`, a TCC grant, a listening-port evidence kind) rather than by rule name, so a finding never appears under a heading its evidence does not support. Each pane lists 12 and states the remainder with the total.\n\nMeasured: still 100.00 on the deterministic audit, and the document shrank from 492 KB to 199 KB because a pane no longer carries all 183 findings.\n\n**Two defects that only looking at the render could find:**\n\n1. `chip-label` with `:style \"fill\"` matched nothing — `dds.css` defines only `\"outlined\"` and `\"text\"`. Every severity chip rendered as uncoloured plain text, i.e. a security tool showing severity nowhere. Neither the test suite nor the 100.00 audit caught it, because both check structure and tokens rather than whether a variant name exists upstream. Now pinned by a test that asserts the value is one DADS defines.\n2. The native window depended on a patched host built into the *shared* shell checkout. Mid-session another agent rebuilt it from unpatched source and the webview silently stopped working. `bin/misogi-build-host` now patches a copy and builds into misogi's own `target/`, never writing into that checkout.\n\n# Closing the CleanMyMac gap (2026-07-27)\n\nOwner direction: implement every remaining item from the comparison, and extract shared libraries into `kotoba-lang` where it makes sense.\n\n**Two libraries extracted**, so the reusable halves stop being product-specific: `kotoba-lang/quarantine` (reversible removal — the pure purge gate is the only thing that can authorise destruction) and `kotoba-lang/macos-inventory` (read-only inventory; no probe may raise a consent dialog, and a probe that cannot see reports `:denied` with a reason). `misogi.host.probe` and `misogi.host.vault` are now thin adapters holding only the mapping and misogi's own judgements.\n\n**All four structurally dead rules now fire.** `:kegare/crash-report` required a trailing slash that the surveyed roots could not produce (99 hits once widened). `:tsumi/kext` had no producer at all (2). `:kegare/dev-build-artifact` and `:kegare/dev-dependency` needed a `:mode :find` root, and no default used one (10 and 14). Findings went 183 -> 275, tsumi 26 -> 55.\n\n**Three items were narrowed on safety grounds, with the owner's agreement, and the narrowing is the design rather than an apology:**\n\n- **Shredder**: `purge --shred` overwrites *the vault's own* bytes before unlinking. There is still no secure-delete for arbitrary user files because there is still no delete opcode. Both the library and the CLI state that overwriting does not certify erasure on APFS — claiming a secure erase that did not happen would be the worst lie this code could tell.\n- **Updater**: detection only. An updater that fetches and executes third-party binaries is a supply-chain component, and putting one inside a tool whose selling point is auditable local behaviour would undo the claim. Each app is routed to its own updater instead.\n- **Antivirus**: heuristics plus Gatekeeper/XProtect state, not an engine. Apple's own blocklist version is surfaced because it matters more than any list misogi ships.\n\n**Maintenance is the one surface that is neither read-only nor reversible-by-vault** — you cannot un-flush a DNS cache. So each task is data stating its effect and its honest `:reversible?`, runs only when acknowledged, and is argv rather than a shell string. Tasks needing root are **refused, not escalated**: misogi does not ask for administrator rights, and a maintenance convenience is not a justification. 3 of 7 are runnable. \"Repair permissions\" is deliberately absent — Apple removed the concept, and offering it in 2026 would be selling reassurance.\n\n**The uninstaller holds two invariants that were tempting to bend.** `/Applications` is not an allow-root, so the app bundle is refused and reported rather than widening the policy for a convenience. And an app's own containers and cookies are `:user-data`, so they stay unselected without `--include-documents` — \"user-data is never auto-selected, except for uninstall\" is an exception big enough to drive a container through. Verified on a real app: the default run admits 0 of Bitwarden's 3 artifacts. The bundle-id matcher is anchored on full path segments because prefix matching is exactly how an uninstaller eats a neighbour's data; `com.foo` never claims `com.foobar`.\n\n**Bounding the survey cost three attempts and is the most transferable lesson here.** Adding system, mail, document and `:find` roots made the first run exceed ten minutes and it had to be killed — `~/github` holds a ~3800-repo west superproject, and each `:find` match feeds a recursive `measure`. Now: a find-match cap (120), a children cap (600), entry-cap lowered 20000 -> 5000, and a hard 300 s wall-clock budget checked between roots and between candidates. Anything cut off is reported as `:partial` with \"stopped at the cap — more exist\", never as clean. And explicit `--find` roots are surveyed **first**: when the defaults ran first they consumed the whole budget and `--find` silently produced nothing, which is precisely the looks-complete-but-isn't failure the coverage reporting exists to prevent.\n\nAlso fixed: `~/Library/Cookies` is TCC-protected and raises EPERM even for its owner, which aborted the whole uninstall. Unreadable artifact roots now degrade and are reported.\n\n120 tests / 381 assertions across the three repos. Console still 100.00 on design-quality with all ten panes.\n\n# Status and resume point (2026-07-27)\n\n**Landed on default branches.** `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi@ef44255`, `kotoba-lang/quarantine@94ddeaf`, `kotoba-lang/macos-inventory@035f88a`, `kotoba-lang/jp-go-digital-design-system` (button `:attrs` + mobile/a11y ext rules, contained in the pinned `f9ef469`). Superproject: west pin `ef44255`, both libraries registered in `repos.edn` and `west.yml`, design-quality ledger seq 178-197.\n\n**Verification actually run:** 99 tests / 291 assertions (misogi core, nbb), 21 / 90 (view, needs the DADS checkouts), 26 / 76 (quarantine, incl. real-filesystem round trips), 19 / 52 (macos-inventory parsers against captured output), 17 / 148 (jp-go-dds). JVM `.cljc` subsets green in misogi and quarantine. Console scores 100.00 on the deterministic design-quality audit with all ten panes; a deliberately bad control page scores 30.96, so that number is discrimination rather than fail-open. Full survey of this machine: 275 findings in ~4-5 min.\n\n**Not done, in the order I would take them:**\n\n1. **Survey cost.** ~4-5 min with everything enabled, bounded only by the 300 s budget and the caps. The cost is `measure` re-walking trees; the fix is to stop recomputing sizes the OS already knows (`APFS` directory sizes / `du`-style cheap paths), not to raise the caps.\n2. **Login items remain `:denied`.** The BTM store is an NSKeyedArchiver blob and `sfltool` prompts for admin. Enumerating them properly needs either an archive decoder or a different API.\n3. **No JS bridge in the webview**, so the hosted console is read-only — the rail navigates and accordions open (pure CSS) but buttons need the CLI. Closing it needs a `WKScriptMessageHandler` in `kotoba-lang/shell`, which is itself blocked behind the unpushed WKWebView WIP on `rescue/wip-20260718`.\n4. **No CI** in any of the three repos.\n5. `:kegare/large-stale`, `:kegare/incomplete-download`, `:kegare/simulator-runtime`, `:kegare/trash`, `:kegare/mail-attachment`, `:tsumi/known-family`, `:tsumi/listening-unsigned`, `:tsumi/unremovable-profile` have never fired on this machine — all reachable, all true negatives or permission-blocked rather than dead. Confirming them needs a machine with the corresponding state.\n\n**First command on resume:** `cd orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi && npm test && ./bin/misogi survey` — then `bin/misogi-build-host` and `bin/misogi-app --shell-window` for the native window (the patched host is gitignored and must be rebuilt after a fresh clone).\n\n# Consequences\n\n- Disk is not reclaimed at `apply` time. Users who expect a cleaner to free space instantly will see space freed only after `purge`. This is deliberate and is surfaced in the UI as vault state, not hidden.\n- The vault costs disk equal to what was quarantined until purged; the default retention floor is 7 days.\n- Full Disk Access is required for the TCC and persistence surveys to be complete. Without it the survey reports reduced coverage explicitly rather than silently under-reporting.\n\n# Alternatives rejected\n\n- **Extend `ai-gftd-mamori`.** Rejected: mamori's core is owner-scoped cloud state over kotoba Datomic; misogi is local-only and must never ship an inventory of a user's disk anywhere.\n- **Unlink with an undo journal.** Rejected: an undo journal that references deleted bytes is not an undo. Move-to-vault is the only restore that actually restores.\n- **Single \"cleanliness\" score.** Rejected: it is precisely the collapse that lets consumer cleaners present a signed-but-stale cache and an unsigned persistence item as the same kind of problem.\n")))
